            <ArticleTitle>What do parents of wheezy children understand by &quot;wheeze&quot;?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>327-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Reported wheeze is the cornerstone of asthma diagnosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine what parents understand by wheeze.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two studies were undertaken: (1) Parents of clinic attendees with reported wheeze (n=160) were asked by questionnaire what they understood by &quot;wheeze&quot; and how they knew their child was wheezy. Responses were compared to definitions of wheeze in 12 epidemiology studies and their response options. (2) The extent of agreement of parents' reports (n=139) of acute wheezing in their children and clinicians' findings of &quot;wheeze&quot; and &quot;asthma&quot; was examined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">(1) &quot;Sound&quot; and &quot;difficulty in breathing&quot; were perceived central to &quot;wheeze&quot;. &quot;What you hear&quot; was not selected by 23% (95% confidence interval (CI) 16-30%). &quot;Whistling&quot; was mentioned by 11% (CI 6-15%) but featured in 11 of 12 epidemiology questionnaires. (2) There was les than 50% agreement between parents' and clinicians' reports of wheeze and asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Conceptual understandings of &quot;wheeze&quot; for parents of children with reported wheeze are different from epidemiology definitions. Parents' reports of acute wheeze and clinicians' findings also differ.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
